; ACCEPTANCE: a concrete host type goes where an INTERFACE is wanted.
;
; `io.ReadAll(r io.Reader)` is the shape 49.9% of Go's declarable-but-unusable
; surface was said to be blocked on. It is not — interfaces.md measured that and
; refuted it — but this is the part that IS an interface, and it costs one
; declared edge:
;
;   (implements ptr-os-File io-Reader …)
;
; AN INTERFACE IS AN EXISTENTIAL TYPE, `∃X. X × Πᵢ(X → Tᵢ)` (Mitchell & Plotkin
; 1988). PACKING one is manufacturing a closure and is refused (callbacks.md
; tier 3). This program does not pack one: it obtains an `*os.File` and hands it
; over, and **Go's own compiler inserts the coercion**. The emitted text is
;
;   b, e2 := io.ReadAll(f)
;
; with no conversion syntax at all — `⟦coerce⟧ = id`, zero emitted characters.
; What the edge buys is our own type checker not refusing a program the host
; accepts.
;
; EVERY EDGE IS HOST-VERIFIED. The Go api manifest lists the EXPORTED API, so an
; interface sealed by an unexported method (`ast.Decl` has `declNode()`) looks
; satisfied by everything — 182 of 1,651 candidates were false, and `go build`
; on the generated assertion file named every one. The relation this program
; relies on is the survivors.
;
; It prints the size of `go.mod`, which is `wc -c`.

(use go/os as gos)
(use go/io as gio)
(use io)

(export main)

(def main (fn ()
  ((gos.Open "go.mod") (fn (f e1)
    (if (gos.err-nil e1)
        ((gio.ReadAll f) (fn (b e2)
          (if (gos.err-nil e2) (io.print-int (len b)) (io.print-int 0))))
        (io.print-int 0))))))
